WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.969
Idea. Your local cinema puts up its ticket price. Some people stop going. Economists ask a simple question: how strongly did buyers react?

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:23.063
Price elasticity of demand, or P E D, compares two percentage changes. The percentage change in quantity demanded goes on top. The percentage change in price goes underneath.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:31.659
Each change gets a sign. A rise is plus. A fall is minus. This matters, because the sign carries into your answer.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:42.447
On a demand curve, a higher price means a lower quantity demanded. One change is plus and the other is minus, so P E D is usually negative.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:52.583
Method. Step one. Write both changes with signs. Say price rises ten per cent and quantity demanded falls fifteen per cent.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:01:01.916
Steps two and three. Quantity on top, price underneath, then divide. Minus fifteen divided by ten is minus one point five.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:16.271
Step four. Round as the question asks and keep the minus sign. P E D has no per cent sign. Then ignore the sign to classify: one point five is above one, so demand is elastic.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:32.273
Sometimes you are given P E D and must find the quantity change. Multiply P E D by the price change. P E D is minus nought point five and price rises eight per cent, so quantity demanded falls four per cent.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:51.169
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2022. Inny cleans windows. He raised his prices by one point six per cent and quantity demanded fell by two point one per cent. Calculate P E D to two decimal places. Two marks.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:58.717
Write the signs. Price rose, so plus one point six. Quantity demanded fell, so minus two point one.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:11.643
Quantity on top. Minus two point one divided by one point six is minus one point three one two five. To two decimal places, that is minus one point three one.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:24.061
Ignore the sign: one point three one is above one, so demand for Inny's service is elastic. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:32.612
Common mistake. A common mistake is putting the formula upside down. Price over quantity gives the wrong number.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:39.385
Another is dropping the minus sign, or adding a per cent sign. Both can cost the answer mark.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:58.143
Your turn. Now you try. A gym raises its monthly fee by six per cent, and the number of members falls by five per cent. Calculate P E D to two decimal places, then say if demand is elastic or inelastic. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:11.861
Answer. Price is plus six, quantity is minus five. Minus five divided by six is minus nought point eight three three three. To two decimal places, minus nought point eight three.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:21.408
Ignore the sign: nought point eight three is below one, so demand is inelastic. Members fell by a smaller percentage than the fee rose.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:30.683
Remember: write the signs, put quantity over price, divide, and round with the minus sign kept. Now try the practice questions.
